  "structure": {
    "block_count": 50,
    "blocks": [
      {
        "block_id": "block_000000",
        "block_index": 0,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h1",
        "text": "7 Practical Ways First-Time Dads Can Support Their Partner and Baby",
        "text_sha256": "9d83dfa61286dc4f6f4301803bbe6ddf7afee60f55af26819550244660f7d3db",
        "word_count": 11
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000001",
        "block_index": 1,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "by Heidi Murkoff , author of What to Expect When You're Expecting.",
        "text_sha256": "fe0f379871761f84993fc97638e1be60dfaee98ef922ce30fb241d275bfed786",
        "word_count": 12
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000002",
        "block_index": 2,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Light copy updates, updates to sourcing, and fact-checked for accuracy.",
        "text_sha256": "ce4b0fa95b50aecf6e7b67e3e57fba87415f35177add6e78e3c831eddc829533",
        "word_count": 10
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000003",
        "block_index": 3,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "father holding baby",
        "text_sha256": "3580a74a259138bfe1ac340f571e5f876911ac314f0dd96b62c6f98812f67fd5",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000004",
        "block_index": 4,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Congratulations on your soon-to-be-a-daddy status — and kudos for thinking about how to help your partner once your bundle arrives. Your awareness of the huge scale of the task of parenting a newborn and your willingness to do your part reflect a growing trend among new dads who were, only a few decades ago, second-string players when it came to caring for an infant.",
        "text_sha256": "3b21e422476791308925f3c4307131b40c7a280a2fcdc79f8b2e11db6bbbb88c",
        "word_count": 64
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000005",
        "block_index": 5,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "These days, more and more fathers are starters who, like you, couldn't be more eager to get some time on the field. Here’s some new-dad advice on plays to consider during the first few weeks of fatherhood.",
        "text_sha256": "a0dcf5721f5ab925d5aa750d96a2b3ff96b509de0ef509282d4d68061f40abaf",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000006",
        "block_index": 6,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Key Takeaways",
        "text_sha256": "cc3f7f0f4513969e418b56fb93ec0876fd96dd6498395b04301f70dbddff8a45",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000007",
        "block_index": 7,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "New dads can help their partner recover by taking on baby duties like diaper changes, baths, night shifts, and bottle feedings to give her rest and strengthen your bond with the baby.",
        "text_sha256": "453e25e1dd33dfc64670914e2bbc1907cce7c67e4d02f868435ab46d2370e80a",
        "word_count": 32
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000008",
        "block_index": 8,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "You can also support her in other ways, such as by handling meals, chores, and visitor management so your partner can focus on healing and adjusting to motherhood.",
        "text_sha256": "03eae03287b14e783bc9c2e71ad196b38d8dfc314eddf3136e36ebe3bef916e2",
        "word_count": 28
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000009",
        "block_index": 9,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Most importantly, stay connected: Communicate openly, accept help from others, and listen to your partner’s needs — teamwork makes those first few weeks smoother for everyone.",
        "text_sha256": "e78aba6800890cbc1574be2200de78f918d332efe14b4abcd90461690411320c",
        "word_count": 26
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000010",
        "block_index": 10,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Chip in where you can. Regardless of how your partner delivers, her body will need time to recover. And breastfeeding is the only baby-care activity limited to moms. Dads can bathe, change diapers , and rock with the best of moms. Doing so allows your partner a break from the action.",
        "text_sha256": "345b716f3bdd03de7da20e569895147d716a0c5e292fd7a949c51e4d5abbc463",
        "word_count": 51
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000011",
        "block_index": 11,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Share bottle duties. If your partner is nursing, once breastfeeding is established there's more than one way to feed your baby. Although you can't breastfeed, you can be the one to give your baby bottles of expressed milk or formula, which gives your partner a break and you extra opportunities for bonding with your baby. Make the most of the moment by striking a nursing position, with your baby snuggled close and the bottle where the breast would be. Opening up your shirt for skin-on-skin contact will enhance the experience for both of you.",
        "text_sha256": "01a2377129a7bd28c517c0a580122e3be97fbc4eea3a3510a2c58c4a9ca7ad75",
        "word_count": 94
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000012",
        "block_index": 12,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Take the night shift. Share in the early weeks of sleepless nights. Even if you're not giving supplementary bottles, there's plenty you can do: Pick baby up, do any necessary diaper changing, deliver baby to your partner for feedings, and return baby to the crib or bassinet once the feeding is finished. Not only will you be connecting more with your baby, you'll also be giving your partner some much-needed rest.",
        "text_sha256": "a6bf9a6c313ee83080df29649aa22797ad903906a3625e2bfd08ee397d9ea88b",
        "word_count": 71
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000013",
        "block_index": 13,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Be a bouncer. Friends and family will no doubt be anxious to meet your brand-new baby, but know when to say no … and hold your ground. If you and your partner are wiped out or just not up to guests yet, feel free to (politely) banish newborn visitors . They’ll understand, and your partner will be grateful they didn’t have to be the banisher.",
        "text_sha256": "72ece8a60dd68b26a254fc1b9798ae4f3a542a7020509879e3fc591821d6d262",
        "word_count": 65
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000014",
        "block_index": 14,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Pick up your partner's shift. It might seem obvious, but here’s an invaluable piece of new-dad advice: Never ask an exhausted new mom, \"What's for dinner?\" Instead, try, \"Hey, what can I fix you for supper?\" Parenthood is more than a full-time job for the first few weeks, which means your partner's share of chores will pile up. So pick up the slack on dishes, laundry, dusting, thank-you notes — whatever needs doing, whenever you can.",
        "text_sha256": "12def5d8b71aef3b34f3cbc92668699c9c3c48dd5cb8c7bee53f0ee869493ebb",
        "word_count": 76
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000015",
        "block_index": 15,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Have more questions about parenting during the newborn stage? You can get answers in the What to Expect app , plus chat with other dads who have been there.",
        "text_sha256": "4e8de72e49f13de6763d5d8b1b2673f1f8d94bc97bfc4abb8b7f01c735798372",
        "word_count": 29
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000016",
        "block_index": 16,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Don't be a martyr. Even household heroes need help sometimes, so accept assistance whenever it’s offered. Let your folks cook or bring in a meal, let a pal do a load of laundry, let your sister change a diaper or take the baby for a stroll. Most importantly, never turn down an opportunity to grab a nap.",
        "text_sha256": "e57c7dfa8eaa1f9a6f8f00202c5177f1d30bfc35fd707df114a79103696d15b1",
        "word_count": 57
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000017",
        "block_index": 17,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Listen up. Lots of people will offer you new-dad advice, but the most important person to listen to once the baby arrives is your partner. Encourage her to tell you how she's feeling and what she needs, and she'll likely do the same for you. Things won’t feel quite so overwhelming if you know your teammate’s got your back.",
        "text_sha256": "810430117fae8b1e1ba00538a4c6c19e497634328e70dd7c561e920340b966ff",
        "word_count": 59
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000018",
        "block_index": 18,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Good luck. Sounds like you’re going to be an MVP (most valuable parent)!",
        "text_sha256": "785deb5aa7e346587c10a4f23637a25a0954f3dc86c7565ee166f90059cf8907",
        "word_count": 13
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000019",
        "block_index": 19,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Heidi Murkoff",
        "text_sha256": "6f05d1b1394d028842497499fff96bffb14810fb4a10d1a23a1ef1b82a72292e",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000020",
        "block_index": 20,
        "block_type": "blockquote",
        "tag": "blockquote",
        "text": "What to Expect the First Year , 3rd edition, Heidi Murkoff. What to Expect When You're Expecting , 5th edition, Heidi Murkoff. WhatToExpect.com, How to Change a Baby’s Diaper , May 2023. WhatToExpect.com, Bottle-Feeding and Supplementation , 2025. WhatToExpect.com, How to Create a Night Shift System When You Have a Newborn , June 2022.",
        "text_sha256": "649c3fbaaa079f0b855156bdab6e16e7c45ca3622172a6527ee7f232cd40941d",
        "word_count": 54
      },
